    "description": "Intercity passenger rail is best understood through specific train services, accommodation, stopping patterns, and ticket conditions. Inspected operator documentation illustrates these distinctions, while leaving nationwide service coverage and performance unresolved.",
    "tags": ["Intercity trains", "Passenger rail", "Compartments", "Rail tickets", "Fadak"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:47:18Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:47:18Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Intercity Trains in Iran: Services and Accommodation | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction to Iranian intercity train accommodation, service information, and the distinction between advertised routes and dated availability."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "understanding-a-service",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Read the service, not just the destination",
        "content": "A city pair alone does not describe a passenger train. Accommodation, stopping pattern, catering, and ticket conditions also matter. Fadak’s official purchase guide illustrates this distinction: it directs users to separate information about train facilities, intermediate stations, and refund conditions within the booking results. This is evidence about the operator’s documented booking process, not a nationwide standard for every railway company. [s1]\n\nThe guide also distinguishes an ordinary compartment booking from reserving a whole compartment. For an international reader, the useful point is that a place on a train and exclusive use of a compartment are different products. The exact arrangement must be established from the selected service rather than inferred from the general appearance of a train. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "compartments-and-marketing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Accommodation and advertised amenities",
        "content": "Fadak describes its trains in terms of compartment accommodation, beds, bedding, entertainment, internet access, and onboard hospitality. Its detailed introduction also describes compartment air conditioning and catering tables. These are operator descriptions of the product being sold. They do not independently establish that every amenity is available on every departure or that passengers consistently receive the advertised experience. [s2, s3]\n\nThe same distinction applies to bundled travel. Fadak describes packages combining rail tickets with hotels and, in some cases, transfers or other destination services. A transfer included in a particular package should not be treated as an automatic feature of a standalone rail ticket. [s3]\n\nThese undated pages were inspected after the requested cutoff. Their descriptions are useful evidence of what the website presented at inspection, but they cannot alone establish the precise product available on 26 October 2026.",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "routes-and-availability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Advertised routes and dated availability",
        "content": "Fadak’s homepage presents Tehran–Mashhad and connections involving Rasht among its prominent routes. These examples demonstrate that its passenger offering is described through named city pairs; they are not a complete inventory of Iranian intercity trains. Nor does a prominent route listing establish daily frequency or a seat available on a particular date. [s2]\n\nThe operator’s documented booking sequence begins with origin, destination, dates, passenger details, and accommodation choices, followed by available results and service information. It then proceeds through passenger identification, review, and payment. This sequence explains why a general route description and a dated reservation answer different questions. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "evidence-needed-for-comparison",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What remains to be established",
        "content": "A national comparison needs more than amenities and route advertising. It requires dated timetables, a verified operator inventory, and evidence about accessibility, reliability, and passenger volumes. None of those outcomes can be inferred from the marketing descriptions inspected here. This draft therefore explains how to interpret service information while leaving comparative judgments and current travel arrangements unresolved.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
    ],
